Q: How to convert non-ASCII hex to char in C? A: <p>Taking into account the first line in your question</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I want to convert any given hex value to a char and then print the actual char string.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>it seems what you need is the following. The user enters a number and the program outputs the hexadecimal representation of the number as a string of characters that represent hexadecimal digits of the entered number.</p>
<p>The second line in your question</p>
<blockquote>
<p>on compiler options)he length of the given hex value <code>0xHHHHHHHH</code> may vary from 1 to 8.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>hints how to determine the size of the character array that will contain the hexadecimal representation of the entered number.</p>
<p>Otherwise your question as written does not make sense. And what your presented code is doing also does not make sense. For example at least the string <strong>&quot;0x1EB&quot;</strong> should be splited into two characters (ignoring the hexadecimal prefix 0x) that are internally represented as <strong>'\x1</strong>' and <strong>'\xEB'</strong>. Pay attention to that <strong>sizeof( char )</strong> always equal to <strong>1</strong> in <strong>C</strong> (and <strong>C++</strong>). So an object of the type character can be represented only with <strong>2</strong> hexadecimal digits. And moreover the type <strong>char</strong> can behave either as type <strong>signed char</strong> or <strong>unsigned char</strong> (depending on compiler options). However even in this case to output such characters as ASCII characters also does not make sense because not all ASCII characters have graphic representations (see also my comments to your question).</p>
<p>So returning to my assumption relative to your actual assignment the corresponding program can look for example the following way.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main( void )
{
    int x;

    printf( &quot;Enter a number: &quot; );

    if ( scanf( &quot;%d&quot;, &amp;x ) == 1 )
    {
        enum { HEX_PREFIX_LEN = 2 };

        char s[2 * sizeof( x ) + HEX_PREFIX_LEN + sizeof( ( char )'0' )] = &quot;0x&quot;;

        size_t n = 0;

        unsigned int tmp = x;

        do 
        { 
            ++n; 
        } while ( ( tmp &gt;&gt;= 4 ) != 0 );

        char *p = s + HEX_PREFIX_LEN + n;

        tmp = x;

        do
        {
            unsigned int digit = tmp &amp; 0x0F;
            *--p = digit &lt; 10 ? digit + '0' : digit - 10 + 'A';
        } while ( ( tmp &gt;&gt;= 4 ) != 0 );

        printf( &quot;%d -&gt; %s\n&quot;, x, s );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The program output might look like</p>
<pre><code>Enter a number: 1024 
1024 -&gt; 0x400
</code></pre>
<p>or like</p>
<pre><code>Enter a number: 11259375 
11259375 -&gt; 0xABCDEF
</code></pre>
<p>At least the demonstration program shows some technique that you may implement in your assignment.</p>

